reasoning:
All three meet the core spec, and all of their own tests pass (X 12, Y 55, Z 60). The ranking comes down to edge cases and robustness.

Z is the most faithful to the spec:
- It reads ids as integers consistently: negatives and huge values give 'not found', and the id is echoed exactly as typed.
- It is the only one that keeps ids from being reused when the counter in the file is inconsistent.
- It does not create the data file on a no-op clear-done.
- It is the only one whose tests actually check the 'sorted by id' line with out-of-order stored data.

Y has the best I/O error handling, the best README and very strong tests. It trails Z narrowly because:
- it reports huge integer ids as 'invalid id' (debatable against the spec);
- clear-done writes even when nothing changed;
- the sort is never really tested;
- the comment at todo.mjs:2 points to files that do not exist.

X is the cleanest and smallest code. It is clearly last because:
- it returns exit 2 'invalid id' for negative integers, which the spec treats as integers;
- it misreports huge ids;
- it dumps raw stack traces with full paths on I/O errors;
- it crashes on malformed items in the file;
- its tests and README are much thinner.
